[00:01.50]Currently,
[00:02.48]great achievements have been made in the field 
[00:05.98]of artificial intelligence,
[00:07.67]which is taking over a good many human jobs.
[00:12.00]In the excerpt,
[00:13.28]the author assumes that since artificial intelligence can plan 
[00:17.28]and organize for us,
[00:19.46]our brains will regress.
[00:21.52]Nevertheless,
[00:22.80]I disagree with the author’s opinion.
[00:25.94]I cling to the idea that our world won’t be like 
[00:28.94]some science-fiction scenes where human brains are inferior,
[00:34.88]even if intelligent machines are getting increasingly smarter.
[00:37.29]To begin with,
[00:38.46]no matter how superior,
[00:40.43]artificial intelligence needs us just as much as we need it. 
[00:45.43]It requires our supplies and repairs,
[00:49.19]hence our brains are going to catch up with the artificial intelligence.
[00:55.18]What is more,
[00:56.26]interactions between humans can never be replaced by machines.
[01:01.37]For intelligent machines,
[01:03.63]it is unlikely to develop human-like social competence
[01:07.63] which consumes a great deal of brain work.
[01:11.68]After all,
[01:12.57]with the development of artificial intelligence,
[01:14.99]human brains are also evolving.
[ti:2-10-材料作文]
